"""Class 10 SST Ch 21 Consumer Rights - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"Consumers need protection in the marketplace.", r"Individual consumers are often in a weak position against large producers and sellers, who may adopt unfair trade practices and short weights.", "a",
     r"The weak position of consumers against powerful sellers is why protection is required; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"The Consumer Protection Act 1986 (COPRA) was an important step for the consumer movement.", r"The Right to Information Act was enacted in October 2005.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but the RTI Act has no bearing on why COPRA was important."),
 AR4(r"Under COPRA, a consumer can appeal to the State and then the National Commission if the case is dismissed by the District Commission.", r"The District, State and National Commissions form a single-tier system.", "c",
     r"A is true. R is false: they form a three-tier quasi-judicial machinery."),
 AR4(r"The ISI mark and Agmark assure the quality of all goods sold in the market.", r"Standardisation marks such as ISI, Agmark and Hallmark help consumers be assured of quality.", "d",
     r"A is false: many goods in the market do not carry these marks. R is true."),
 AR4(r"Consumers have the right to be informed about the ingredients, price and date of manufacture of goods.", r"Information helps the consumer to complain and ask for a replacement or compensation if the goods are defective.", "a",
     r"Knowing the particulars is what allows a consumer to claim a remedy; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Residents' welfare associations and consumer organisations help consumers.", r"The consumer movement in India arose in an organised form in the 1960s against adulteration, black marketing and food shortage.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but R does not explain A."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Abirami joined a two-year course at a coaching institute, but after one year found the teaching poor and asked for a refund of one year's fee. The institute refused. She went to the District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission, which directed the institute to refund Rs 28,000, saying she had the right to choose. The institute appealed to the State Commission, which upheld the order and fined it for a frivolous appeal. "
   r"(i) Which consumer right did Abirami use? (ii) At which level did she first file her case? (iii) What did the State Commission do? (iv) Why was it important that she had the right to leave the service?",
   r"(i) Right to choose (1). (ii) The District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (1). (iii) It upheld the District Commission's order and fined the institute for a frivolous appeal (1). (iv) Every consumer, whatever the service, has the right to decide whether to continue to receive it; without it a consumer could be forced to go on paying for poor service (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A shopkeeper sells a packed biscuit at a higher price than the printed MRP and says that he can sell it only with a second product that Ritu does not want. The packet does not show the date of manufacture. Ritu knows the three-tier system of consumer courts and decides to complain about a claim of Rs 8 lakh. "
   r"(i) Which right is denied when she is forced to take another item? (ii) Where should she file a complaint under COPRA about a claim of Rs 8 lakh? (iii) What does MRP stand for? (iv) Why must manufacturers print details such as date of manufacture on packets?",
   r"(i) Right to choose (1). (ii) The District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission, as claims up to Rs 1 crore go to it (1). (iii) Maximum Retail Price (1). (iv) Consumers have the right to be informed, so they can judge the quality and the period of use, and can complain or seek replacement or compensation if the goods are defective or expired (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Sunil bought a gas stove. It had no ISI mark, and the burner exploded while cooking. He also noticed that the packet of edible oil he had bought carried the Agmark logo. A neighbour told him that these logos and certificates help consumers assure themselves of quality. "
   r"(i) Which right did Sunil lose when the defective stove caused an injury? (ii) Name the agricultural certification mark mentioned. (iii) Name one more certification mark used for gold jewellery. (iv) Why is government action as well as consumer awareness needed to maintain quality?",
   r"(i) Right to safety (1). (ii) Agmark (1). (iii) Hallmark (1). (iv) Producers may not follow quality rules unless the rules are supervised; the consumer movement is also not strong enough, so both are needed for good quality (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

MISTAKES = [
 r"Listing the rights incorrectly: <b>choice, information, redressal, representation, safety and consumer education</b> are the six rights in COPRA.",
 r"Mixing up the three-tier system: <b>District</b> (up to Rs 1 crore), <b>State</b> (Rs 1 crore to Rs 10 crore), <b>National</b> (above Rs 10 crore).",
 r"Treating ISI, Agmark and Hallmark as interchangeable: <b>ISI</b> for industrial goods, <b>Agmark</b> for agricultural goods, <b>Hallmark</b> for gold jewellery.",
 r"Writing that the Consumer Protection Act was passed in 1960 or 2005: COPRA was enacted in <b>1986</b> (amended in 2019), the RTI Act in <b>October 2005</b>.",
 r"Thinking that consumers can complain only to the shopkeeper; they can also go to the Consumer Disputes Redressal Commissions and can file a <b>class action</b> or be represented by consumer organisations.",
 r"Confusing the right to be <b>informed</b> (MRP, date of manufacture, ingredients) with the right to <b>choose</b> (not being forced to buy a product or service).",
]
